        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
To study the parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) expression in bones from animals exposed to simulated and microgravity.<P>

APPROACH<br>
Lung cells were isolated and cultured from Sprague-Dawley rats. Lung cells were transfected with a PTHrP-GFP promoter. UMR-106 RAT Osteocarcinoma Cells were obtained from a culture bank. Human UMR 106 bone cells were also used. Cultured cells were exposed to simulated microgravity using a Rotating Wall Vessel Bioreactor for 72 hours. PTHrP levels were measured by ELISA. Gene expression was measured by northern RT-PCR and GFP fluorescence in lung cells, and RT-PCR in human bone cells. Cells were also subjected to a return to 1g and PTHrP was similarly characterized. Femurs, tibias, and parietal bones from skulls of rats flown in space on mission STS-58 were also obtained. Spongy tissue from these bone was harvested 1-day post flight and maintained at -70C. Expression of PTHrP was characterized using RT-PCR and compared to bones from matched ground control animals.<P>

RESULTS:<br>
The experiment results were found to be consistent with the hypothesis that microgravity down-regulates PTHrP. Following exposure to simulated microgravity in the Rotating Wall Vessel Bioreactor, PTHrP expression in lung cells was decreased by 80-90% in the initial 8 hours and maintained this level through the final timepoint of 72 hours. Similar results were seen in the human bone cell culture, decreasing 64% in the initial 8 hours, decreasing an additional 10% over the next 4 hours, and maintaining this level through the final 72-hour timepoint of the experiment. RT-PCR from tibia and femur samples from animals flown in space for 2 weeks showed an 85%-90% decrease in PTHrP expression compared to the ground control samples. Contrastingly, there was no difference in expression of PTHrP in parietal skull bones between flight and ground samples. This lead to the conclusion that PTHrP expression is downregulated as a result of microgravity specifically in weight-bearing bones.
        </div>

